\section{Introduction}

% What's the problem?
We receive emails from students asking why they haven't gotten their grades 
reported yet.
% Why is it a problem? Research gap left by other approaches?
Since all grades are reported automatically, this means that there is something 
they haven't completed.
% Why is it important? Why care?
We still have to answer these emails, which takes time.
So we want to reduce the time it takes to answer these emails.

% What's the approach? How to solve the problem?
Our approach is to write utilities that parse the email and generate the needed 
commands to find the students results.
We need to look these up to answer the question and it's good to send these to 
the student so that they see.
% What's the findings? How was it evaluated, what are the results, limitations, 
% what remains to be done?

In this era, a first thought might be that this would require the use of an LLM 
to read the email and generate the commands to fetch the results.
(Or that the LLM simply calls those commands directly.)
That would be a complete waste of energy since we can do something that is more 
efficient and more deterministic (more sustainable and less error prone).

This is the literate source code of the [[<<[[edumail.sh]]>>]] bash script.
The idea is that it's a command that works like Git with different subcommands.
Then we simply call it when we are processing a student email.
We'll use a simple construction to achieve this.

\subsection{Structural overview}

We'll start with a [[<<[[edumail.sh]]>>]] script that contains the main 
function.
This function will parse the first argument as the subcommand to call.
Then it will call that subcommand function with the rest of the arguments.
\begin{minted}{bash}
edumail results email.txt
\end{minted}
This calls the [[results]] function with the argument [[email.txt]].

We'll execute the [[main]] function if the script is not sourced\footnote{%
  Sourced: included in another script as a library.%
}.
This way, we can source the script and use the functions directly in the 
current shell.
So instead of \mintinline{bash}{edumail results email.txt}, we can do
\begin{minted}{bash}
source edumail.sh
results email.txt
\end{minted}

We'll use a rather standard structure for the script.
<<[[edumail.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash

<<variables>>
<<functions>>

main() {
  <<parse which subcommand should be called and call its function>>
}

# call main if not sourced
# https://stackoverflow.com/a/28776166/1305099
(return 0 2>/dev/null) || main "$@"
@

\subsection{Invoke right subcommand function}

The first argument ([[$1]]) should be the subcommand.
This should be the same as the name of its function.
This way we can simply shift the arguments and call that function,
then it can parse the rest of the arguments as it sees fit.
However, an easier way in this case is to simply evaluate [["$@"]],
this would have the same effect as shifting the arguments and calling the 
function.

If no subcommand is given, we print a usage message.
This should include a list of all subcommands.
It should look like this:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", ""], capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

For this we use a trick of using the side effects of boolean operators:
[[bash]] only evaluates what is needed---for an \enquote{and} operator 
([[&&]]) we don't evaluate the second if the first is false (can't get two true 
values if the first is false), for the \enquote{or} operator ([[||]]) we don't 
evaluate the second if the first is true (we already have a true value, so the 
second can't change the outcome).
<<parse which subcommand should be called and call its function>>=
[[ "$1" = "" ]] \
  && <<echo help text>> \
  || "$@"
<<echo help text>>=
echo -e "Usage: $0 [function] args\nFunctions:\n<<list all functions>>"
@

To get a list of all the functions, we simply grep them out of the script 
itself, given by [[$0]].
We skip the [[main]] function, since that one it not interesting.
<<list all functions>>=
$(sed -En 's/^(\w+)\(\) \{/  \1/p' $0 \
  | grep -Pv '^\s*main$')
@

\section{The results subcommands}

We want a set of subcommands that can be used to get a student's results from 
the course in question.
It will not get the student's actual results, but rather return command lines 
that can fetch the results.

\begin{example}\label{email1}
An email from a student\footnote{%
  The student's identity is anonymized and the fictitious name has no 
  relation to any of the student's attributes (other than being a 
  student).%
} may look like the following.
The lines that are marked contain useful data.
\inputminted[highlightlines={2,5,11-12,14}]{text}{email1.txt}
This email should then yield something like the following output:
\begin{minted}{bash}
ladok student -c DD1317 -r \
  $(canvaslms users -sc DD1317 "Studenten Studentensson" -l | cut -f 3)
canvaslms submissions -c DD1317 \
  -u "Studenten Studentensson"
kthutils forms export restlabb23/24 \
  | grep "Studenten Studentensson"
\end{minted}
\end{example}
The [[ladok]] command fetches the student's results from LADOK.
However, to do that, we need the student's identifier from Canvas.
We can use [[canvaslms]] to fetch student data from Canvas.
Restlabb takes presentations for the intro programming courses after the course 
has ended.
Those TAs can't access the course's Canvas room, so they file their reports in 
a form that can be exported as a spreadsheet.
The [[kthutils forms export]] command can export those results for us.

\begin{example}\label{email2}
Another email:
\inputminted[highlightlines={2,5,12}]{text}{email2.txt}
This email should then yield the following output:
\begin{minted}{bash}
ladok student -c XXX -r \
  $(canvaslms users -sc ".*" "^studenten@kth.se" -l | cut -f 3)
canvaslms submissions -c ".*" \
  -u "^studenten@kth.se" \
  -a "granskning"
kthutils forms export restlabb23/24 \
  | grep "Studenten Studentensdotter"
\end{minted}
\end{example}

Why do we want it to output those commands and not the results directly?
We use [[mutt]] for reading and replying to emails.
The [[mutt]] email client uses the user's favourite editor to write the 
reply---Vim.
The idea is then that when we're writing the reply in Vim, we do
\begin{quote}
  [[.!edumail.sh results %]]
\end{quote}
on a blank line to replace the blank line with the output (the commands).
(The [[%]] is a Vim placeholder for the current file, which is the email.)
Then we can select the relevant lines and execute them in the shell:
\begin{quote}
  [['<,'>!bash]]
\end{quote}
If needed, we can edit the lines first---this is why we want the command lines 
and not the results directly.

So, we should be able to pass a filename as argument (so that we can pass 
[[%]]).
That file should contain the email as shown in the examples above.
And it should generate the output as shown in the examples above to stdout.

We'll add a subcommand for each of the lines in the output above.
One that focuses on the [[ladok]] command, one on [[canvaslms]] and the last on 
restlabb.
Then we'll let the [[results]] subcommand call all of these subcommands.

Tools like [[grep]] and [[sed]] are very useful because they either take a file 
as input or read from stdin.
So while we in most cases can read the email from a file, we also want to be 
able to pipe the email to the script.

The subcommand functions will usually be quite straight forward in considering 
this, but for the [[results]] subcommand we need each subcommand called to get 
the same input.
So we must ensure that each of the commands can read stdin, which we do with 
the function~[[file_or_stdin]] (we'll get back to it later).
<<functions>>=
results() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  shift
  ladok "$file" "$@"
  echo "echo"
  missing "$file" "$@"
  echo "echo"
  restlabb "$file" "$@"
  echo "echo"
  canvas "$file" "$@"
  echo "echo"
  report "$file" "$@"
}
@

The function [[ladok]] will hide the [[ladok]] command from us, so that we 
can't use it.
However, this won't be an issue since we don't need to invoke it in this 
script.
We simply write [[ladok]] in the output.
And the execution will happen later in the editor.

\subsection{Overall algorithm}

All the subcommands will have to parse the email.
It's just that they try to get different data out of it.

The most efficient approach would be to parse the email only once.
During that round we extract all the data we need.
Then each of the functions can use that data to construct the output.

However, considering that we might call the subcommands directly, each 
subcommand must ensure the email has been parsed.
This speaks for a more independent design of each and, unfortunately, results 
in the email being parsed multiple times.
This has other advantages, such that in a future design of [[results]], we might 
check which of the subcommands are actually needed.

We'll write functions that extract different data from the email.
These can later be used in the subcommands to construct the output.

\subsection{Function design}

The functions will be designed to take a file as argument.
Or if the email is piped to the script, they will read from stdin.
To achieve this we can rely on the same idea as in the UNIX commands:
if they don't get any file as argument, they read from stdin.
(We can use [[/dev/stdin]] to read from stdin, but keep the structure of the 
code.)
Usually this will work, since we only parse the email once in each function.

However, sometimes we need to read stdin twice.
Then we need to keep a copy of the email in a temporary file.
We can do this by checking if the file argument is an empty string.
If it is, we create a temporary file and read from stdin into that file.
Then we pass the temporary file to the function.
<<functions>>=
file_or_stdin() {
  local file="$1"
  if [[ "$file" = "" ]]; then
    file=$(mktemp)
    cat > "$file"
  fi
  echo "$file"
}
@


\section{Extracting the student's identifier}

We'll start by extracting the student's name or institutional email address.
We prefer the institutional email address, but if that's not available we'll 
use the name.
The function will take the email file as an argument---however, if that is en 
empty string, we want to use stdin.
Since we want to use [[student_name]] if [[student_id]] fails, we need to 
ensure that the email file is passed or piped to both functions.
<<functions>>=
student() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  student_id "$file" \
  || student_name "$file" \
  || any_email "$file"
}
@

To extract the email address, we want to extract the line starting with 
\enquote{To: }.
Then we want to extract the email address from that line.
This is because we're doing this when we reply to the email, thus the student's 
name and email are in the \enquote{To: } field.
We'll try to match a KTH email address.
<<functions>>=
student_id() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local email
  email=$(grep "^To: " "$file" \
          | sed -En "s/^To: [^<]**<?(${USER_FORMAT}@${DOMAIN})>?.*$/\1/p")
  if [[ -n "$email" ]]; then
    echo "$email"
    return 0
  else
    return 1
  fi
}
<<variables>>=
DOMAIN="([a-zA-Z0-9]+\.)?kth\.se"
USER_FORMAT="[a-zA-Z0-9]+"
@

If we didn't find an email address, we'll try to extract the student's name.
If the student sent the email through Canvas, we will not have their KTH email 
and we will have the text \enquote{via Canvas Notifications} appended to the 
name---we want to remove that.
<<functions>>=
student_name() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local name
  name=$(grep "^To: " $file | sed 's/^To: //' \
         | sed -E "s/( via Canvas( Notifications)?)? <.*//")
  if [[ -n "$name" ]]; then
    echo "$name"
    return 0
  else
    return 1
  fi
}
@

If we didn't find any useful data in the email headers,
we can also look for the email address anywhere in the email (body).
This is useful when we pipe text to the script, since then we don't necessarily 
include the headers.
<<functions>>=
any_email() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local email
  email=$(grep -Po "${USER_FORMAT}@${DOMAIN}" "$file" | head -n 1)
  if [[ -n "$email" ]]; then
    echo "$email"
    return 0
  else
    return 1
  fi
}
@

Let's test this on the emails from the examples above.
Running [[student]] on \cref{email1} should yield the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "student", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
Running [[student_id]] on \cref{email2} should yield the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "student", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}


\section{Extracting the course}

We'll now extract the course from the email.
It's probably too difficult to try to extract the course title.
But we can for sure extract the course code, since that is on a very specific 
format (\eg DD1317).
We can also extract the course's short name, in the case of DD1317 during 2023 
it would be prgi23.

\subsection{Cleaning irrelevant headers}

There are some lines that we don't want to match anything on.
For example, we don't want to match the email identifier in the [[In-Reply-To]] 
header and not the year in the [[Date]] header.
For this, we construct a filter which removes these lines.
<<functions>>=
clean() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  grep -vE "^(${IGNORE_HEADERS})" "$file"
}
<<variables>>=
IGNORE_HEADERS="In-Reply-To:|References:|Message-ID:|Content-Type:"
IGNORE_HEADERS="${IGNORE_HEADERS}|^MIME-Version:|^X-Mailer:"
IGNORE_HEADERS="${IGNORE_HEADERS}|^User-Agent:|Date:"
@

We also don't want to match the reply line.
<<variables>>=
IGNORE_HEADERS="${IGNORE_HEADERS}|On .* wrote:$"
@

\subsection{Extracting course identifiers from clean email}

This leaves us with the following construction.
We first match for course codes, then for course nick names.
Finally, we also try to guess the course based on keywords in the email.
Let's look at the example emails again.
\Cref{email1} should yield something like
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email2} should yield something like
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

Since any matches may occur several times in the email, we'll sort them and 
remove duplicates.
And to deal with output containing several lines, we'll make a disjunct regex 
with [[make_disjunct_regex]] discussed below.
<<functions>>=
course() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  (course_code "$file"; echo; course_nick "$file") \
  | sort -u \
  | make_disjunct_regex
}
@ This function output what we just saw above.
The [[course]] function calls [[course_code]] and [[course_nick]] to extract 
the course code and course nick name, respectively.
Their outputs are just concatenated.

The [[course_code]] function will look for the course code in the email.
If it finds something that looks like a course code, it will return it 
(printing it to stdout).
Then it will look for keywords in the email that indicate which course it is, 
then output the course code for that course.
Since many might match, we sort the output and remove duplicates.
Finally, we make a disjunct regex from the output.
<<functions>>=
course_code() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  (
    clean "$file" \
    | grep -Po "${COURSE_CODE}"
    if <<test for datintro terms>>; then
      echo "DD1301"
      echo "DD1337"
    fi
    if <<test for programming terms>>; then
      echo "DD1310"
      echo "DD1317"
    fi
    if <<test for crypto terms>>; then
      echo "DD2520"
    fi
    if <<test for science terms>>; then
      echo "DA2215"
    fi
  ) | sort -u \
    | make_disjunct_regex
}
@ If we run this function on the examples we get the following.
\Cref{email1} yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course_code", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email2} yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course_code", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

The [[course_nick]] function will do exactly the same, but look for the course 
nick name in the email and output a nickname instead.
<<functions>>=
course_nick() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  (
    clean "$file" \
    | grep -Po "${COURSE_NICK}"
    if <<test for datintro terms>>; then
      echo "datintro${YEARS_REGEX}"
      echo "progd${YEARS_REGEX}"
    fi
    if <<test for programming terms>>; then
      echo "prgm${YEARS_REGEX}"
      echo "prgi${YEARS_REGEX}"
    fi
    if <<test for crypto terms>>; then
      echo "tilkry${YEARS_REGEX}"
    fi
    if <<test for science terms>>; then
      echo "vetcyb${YEARS_REGEX}"
    fi
  ) | sort -u \
    | make_disjunct_regex
}
@ Running the [[course_nick]] function on the examples yields the following.
\Cref{email1} yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course_nick", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email2} yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course_nick", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

For the patterns, the important part is to do a lookahead to see that we don't 
match substrings of other data.
For instance, students usually write the course code with lower case and that 
can be mistaken for a course nick name.
So we want a lookahead to ensure we get space or punctuation after the course 
code.
<<variables>>=
COURSE_CODE="[A-Za-z]{2,3}[0-9]{3,4}[A-Za-z]?(?![A-Za-z0-9])"
COURSE_NICK="[a-z]+[0-9]{2}(?![A-Za-z0-9])"
@ Note that we need the [[-P]] option for that lookahead in the regex to work 
with [[grep]].

Usually the students omit the year they took the course, particularly if they 
took it the last one or two year.
(Those from many years ago tend to write when they took it.)
We need the year so that we can match the course nick name with the last few 
years (instead of all throughout time) when the student gives only the course 
but not the year they took it.
<<variables>>=
YEAR=$(date +%y)
YEARS_REGEX="($((YEAR-2))|$((YEAR-1))|$YEAR)"
@

A lot of the time, the students don't include the course code or nick name in 
the email.
So we have to guess what course it is based on the content of the email.
For the programming course, there are a few things to look for.
Firstly, everything is in Swedish.
Secondly, we look for common terms for the course (programming, Python) and 
then the unique terms for assignments (\enquote{datorprov} as a specific name 
rather than \enquote{labbar}, the latter being too generic).
The crypto and science courses are for fourth and fifth year students, so they 
are in English---although Swedish students sometimes write their emails in 
Swedish if they have figured out that I'm fluent in Swedish.
<<test for programming terms>>=
   <<look for>> "programm?ering|python|DD1310|DD1317" \
|| <<look for>> "datorprov|projekt|p-uppgift|gransk|spec" \
|| <<look for>> "lab(b|oration) [1-6]|Excel" \
|| <<look for>> "restlab"
<<test for datintro terms>>=
   <<look for>> "data?intro|DD13(01|37)" \
|| <<look for>> "ssh|terminal|student-shell|bash|shell|Public" \
|| <<look for>> "rättning|s[ck]ript" \
|| <<look for>> "git|latex"
<<test for crypto terms>>=
   <<look for>> "[kc]rypto|DD2520" \
|| <<look for>> "ciphertext|plaintext|breaking|[ck]rypo?tanalys" \
|| <<look for>> "AES|ProVerif|usability" \
|| <<look for>> "INL1 ?(Written|Oral)"
<<test for science terms>>=
<<look for>> "DA2215|TAMOS|scien|project|seminar|formal|report"
@

We want the [[grep]] to be quiet and case insensitive.
We also want to use the Perl style regex syntax ([[-P]]), as mentioned above.
We'll need to clean the email from irrelevant headers before we look for the 
keywords.
<<look for>>=
clean "$file" | grep -qiP
@

\subsection{Creating a disjunct regex}

To make a disjunct regex, we simply concatenate the lines with a pipe (a sign 
for \enquote{or} in regex).
\begin{example}\label{disjunct-regex-example}
We want to turn
\begin{minted}{text}
DD1317
prgi23
\end{minted}
into
\begin{minted}{text}
(DD1317|prgi23)
\end{minted}
\end{example}
There are two special cases to consider:
We must make sure to not end with a pipe.
And if there is a blank line, we'll get two pipes in a row, which we also want 
to avoid.
So we simply remove these two cases when they occur.
<<functions>>=
make_disjunct_regex() {
  echo -n "("
  tr '\n' '|' | sed 's/||/|/g' | sed 's/|$//'
  echo -n ")"
}
@ Let's test this on \cref{disjunct-regex-example} above.
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(
  "(echo DD1317; echo prgi23) | ./edumail make_disjunct_regex",
  shell=True, capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

If we run this on the emails from the examples above, we should get the 
following output.
Running [[course]] on \cref{email1} yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
This is correct.
The student gave the course code, but not which course round.
The student also named some keywords for the programming courses.
(The years in the regex are calculated from the current year, when we compile 
this document, but the email examples are older than that---from when I wrote 
the first version of this script.
But as long as I read the student's email within a year or two, the regex will 
likely match the course round.)

Running [[course]] on \cref{email2} should yield the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
This is correct, since the email doesn't contain any course code.
Infact, \cref{email2} doesn't identify any course at all.


\section{Extracting assignments}

We also want to extract the assignments.
There are two alternatives: individual assignments or assignment groups (LADOK 
modules).

\subsection{Assignment groups}

The assignment groups are the easiest to extract, they're always named 
something like LAB1, LAB2, LAB3, \etc.
We can match those with a regex.
<<variables>>=
ASSIGNMENT_GROUP="[A-Z][A-Z][A-Z]+[0-9]+"
@

Now we'll do like we've done before and grep for these in the email, getting 
only the matching parts.
<<functions>>=
assignment_groups() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local groups=$( (<<find assignment groups in [[file]]>>) \
                  | sort -u )
  if [[ -n "$groups" ]]; then
    echo "$groups"
  else
    echo "^${ASSIGNMENT_GROUP_DEFAULT}"
  fi | make_disjunct_regex
}
<<variables>>=
ASSIGNMENT_GROUP_DEFAULT="(LAB|INL|SEM)[0-9]+"
@

The first thing we'll do is to look for things that look like assignment groups 
in the email.
We note that we must not match the course code, since those also fit the 
pattern in [[ASSIGNMENT_GROUP]], so we remove anything that also match a course 
code.
<<find assignment groups in [[file]]>>=
clean "$file" \
| grep -Po "${ASSIGNMENT_GROUP}" \
| grep -Pv "${COURSE_CODE}"
@

This will rarely be sufficient, so we also look for keywords that indicate 
which assignment group it is.
This is easy for the datintro and vetcyb courses, they only have one assignment 
group, so we can reuse the tests from above.
However, a keyword in one course might indicate a different assignment group in 
another course, so we'd better make these course specific.
<<find assignment groups in [[file]]>>=
if course "$file" | grep -qP "(DD13(01|37)|datintro)"; then
  if <<test for datintro terms>>; then
    echo "LAB1"
  fi
fi
if course "$file" | grep -qP "(DA2215|vetcyb)"; then
  if <<test for science terms>>; then
    echo "INL1"
  fi
fi
@ Note that we don't use [[elif]]: a student might email about several courses 
in one email.

For the other courses we must be more specific, since they have different 
modules corresponding to different assignments.
<<find assignment groups in [[file]]>>=
if course "$file" | grep -qP "(DD131[07]|prg[im])"; then
  if <<test for programming LAB1 terms>>; then
    echo "LAB1"
  fi
  if <<test for prgoramming LAB2 terms>>; then
    echo "LAB2"
  fi
  if <<test for programming LAB3 terms>>; then
    echo "LAB3"
  fi
  if <<test for programming KAL1 terms>>; then
    echo "KAL1"
  fi
fi
if course "$file" | grep -qP "(DD2520|tilkry)"; then
  if <<test for tilkry LAB1 terms>>; then
    echo "LAB1"
  fi
  if <<test for tilkry INL1 terms>>; then
    echo "INL1"
  fi
fi
@

LAB1 for the programming course is a series of labs.
<<test for programming LAB1 terms>>=
<<look for>> "lab(b|oration) [1-6]"
@ LAB2 is the so called Datorprov, an exam.
<<test for prgoramming LAB2 terms>>=
<<look for>> "datorprov|(kontroll|kort)skrivning|prov|tenta"
@ LAB3 is the project assignment.
<<test for programming LAB3 terms>>=
<<look for>> "projekt|p-uppgift|gransk|spec"
@ KAL1 is the Excel part, consists of four labs in Excel.
<<test for programming KAL1 terms>>=
<<look for>> "Excel"
@

Let's look at the security courses.
LAB1 for applied crypto (tilkry) is a series of mandatory and optional labs.
But it also contains the usabilty and design considerations seminars.
<<test for tilkry LAB1 terms>>=
<<look for>> "AES|ProVerif|usabilty|design|consideration|VSD|seminar"
@ INL1 on that course consists of the creatively named assignments
\begin{itemize}
  \item INL1 Written,
  \item INL1 Oral, and
  \item INL1 Quiz.
\end{itemize}
<<test for tilkry INL1 terms>>=
<<look for>> "INL1 ?(Written|Oral|Quiz)|quiz|written|oral"
@

None of the examples (\cref{email1,email2}) contain any assignment groups.
But they do contain individual assignments.
\begin{example}[Assignment groups]\label{email3}
We can consider the following email that contains assignment groups.
\inputminted[highlightlines={2,5,13}]{text}{email3.txt}
This email should then yield the following output:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms submissions \
  -c ".*" \
  -u "^studuser@kth.se" \
  -A LAB2
\end{minted}
\end{example}

Let's test [[assignment_groups]] on \cref{email3} above.
It yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "assignment_groups", "email3.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

Let's also try it on \cref{email1} above.
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "assignment_groups", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
So we see that it's important to have a good default.
And \cref{email2}:
\begin{pycode}
